Loom: cross-block memory waits use the source alias summaries.

The wait planner's cross-block memory frontier tracks pending accesses per memory space only, so the first
global load of a block drained every store issued earlier, including stores to buffers the source declares
noalias, and the drain also caught the block's own newer stores. gen_gdn_ss: a full s_waitcnt_vscnt 0 before
each chunk's v conv taps behind 15 fresh O stores (3.4% of the row SIMDs' time).

- Relative intervals record when their disjoint-storage identity is an entry argument of the scope function
  (one storage value per invocation); may-alias then trusts unequal identities across evaluations too.
- The wait planner marks a node read (write) independent when no write (read) anywhere in the function may
  alias its dependency accesses, and skips the cross-block drain for it. Opaque counter-backed accesses
  (no descriptor effects, volatile) keep everything conservative.
- DS cross-lane ops (ds_swizzle) touch no memory: their read effect moves from GENERIC to NONE, keeping the
  LGKM counter for their results; no more vscnt drain before a swizzle.

gen_gdn_ss part a -2%, bit-identical.
